micro_lesson.content:
When working with instance attributes, beginners often confuse instance attributes with class attributes, or forget to use self. to bind a value to a specific object. Another frequent error is trying to access an instance attribute before it has been set inside __init__, or shadowing an instance attribute with a local variable inside a method. Always remember: instance attributes belong to a specific object and are created with self.name = value inside __init__. If you see an AttributeError, check whether the attribute was assigned with self. or if you accidentally used a class-level assignment instead.

Example of a correct instance attribute:
class Dog:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

Example of a common error (forgetting self):
class Dog:
    def __init__(self, name):
        name = name  # This creates a local variable, not an instance attribute!

When debugging, print the object's __dict__ to inspect its current instance attributes.

