micro_lesson.content:
Runtime errors often occur when you try to access an instance attribute that was never initialized, or when you accidentally delete it during execution. A typical scenario is calling a method before __init__ has run, or using del self.attr inside a method and then accessing it later. Another runtime pitfall is mixing up instance attributes with parameters: if you forget self., the attribute exists only inside __init__ and is not part of the object.

To avoid runtime AttributeErrors, always ensure that every attribute you plan to read is assigned with self. inside __init__, or guard access with hasattr(self, 'attr').

Example of safe runtime access:
class Safe:
    def __init__(self):
        self.x = 10

    def show(self):
        if hasattr(self, 'x'):
            print(self.x)
        else:
            print('x is missing')

